Performance & Troubleshooting Q&A
1. Your API response time suddenly increases from 200ms to 5 seconds. How would you investigate the root cause?
Check monitoring dashboards (Grafana/APM) first to see if it's affecting all endpoints/users or just one — this narrows scope immediately. Correlate the spike timing with 
recent deployments, config changes, or traffic surges. Check CPU/memory/GC metrics for resource saturation. Check DB query performance and connection pool stats — slow 
queries or pool exhaustion are common culprits. Check downstream/external service calls for added latency. Take a thread dump if threads appear stuck. Review logs with 
trace IDs to pinpoint exactly where time is being spent in the request lifecycle. Once the layer (app, DB, network, downstream) is identified, drill deeper — e.g., 
EXPLAIN ANALYZE for slow queries, or circuit breaker metrics for a failing dependency. Fix immediate cause, then add alerting to catch it earlier next time.

2. A downstream microservice is slow. How do you prevent it from affecting your entire application?
Use resilience patterns: set aggressive timeouts so calls don't hang indefinitely; add a circuit breaker (Resilience4j) to stop calling the failing service once error/latency 
thresholds are breached, allowing it to recover; use bulkheads to isolate thread pools per downstream dependency so one slow service doesn't exhaust all available threads; 
implement fallbacks (cached data, default response) to degrade gracefully instead of failing completely; add retries with exponential backoff + jitter for transient 
failures only, capped to avoid overload. Combined, these ensure the slowness is contained to that one dependency rather than cascading into a full outage of your application.

3. An API works perfectly with 100 users but fails with 10,000 concurrent users. What would you check first?
First check connection pool sizes (DB, HTTP clients) — under low load they're sufficient, but at scale they get exhausted, causing timeouts/failures. 
Check thread pool configuration (Tomcat's max-threads) — may be too small for the concurrency level. Check database performance — locking, slow queries, or 
connection saturation under concurrent writes. Check rate limiting/throttling at the gateway — might be silently dropping requests. Check memory/GC behavior — 
high concurrency can cause memory pressure and long GC pauses. Check downstream dependencies for their own capacity limits. Also verify the app is stateless and 
horizontally scalable — if not, a single instance is likely the bottleneck. Load testing (JMeter/Gatling) in a staging environment helps reproduce and pinpoint the 
exact breaking point before fixing in production.

4. CPU usage is normal, but API latency is very high. What could be happening?
If CPU isn't the bottleneck, latency is likely caused by waiting, not computing. Common causes: slow database queries or lock contention (threads waiting on DB, 
not burning CPU). Connection pool exhaustion — requests queued waiting for an available DB/HTTP connection. Slow downstream/external API calls with no timeout, blocking 
threads. Thread pool saturation — requests queued behind a limited thread pool even though individual threads are idle-waiting. I/O bottlenecks — disk or 
network latency (e.g., slow disk writes, high network round-trip time). GC pauses — occasional long stop-the-world pauses that spike latency without raising average CPU. 
Take a thread dump during a latency spike — if threads show WAITING/BLOCKED states rather than RUNNABLE, it confirms an I/O or lock-based bottleneck rather than a CPU-bound one.

5. How would you identify whether a performance issue is caused by application code, the database, network latency, or a downstream service?
Use distributed tracing (Zipkin/Jaeger/OpenTelemetry) — it breaks down a single request's total time into spans per component (app logic, DB call, downstream call), 
immediately showing where time is spent. If no tracing exists, correlate logs with a trace ID and log timestamps at each stage (before/after DB call, before/after 
downstream call) to manually compute time spent per layer. Check DB-level metrics (query execution time, EXPLAIN ANALYZE) to rule in/out the database. 
Check APM code-level profiling to see if time is in application logic (loops, serialization). Check network metrics (latency, packet loss) between services, especially 
cross-region calls. This systematic, layer-by-layer isolation — rather than guessing — is the fastest way to correctly attribute the bottleneck.

6. One SQL query takes 10 seconds in production but runs quickly in the development environment. How would you investigate?
The most common cause is data volume difference — production has far more rows, exposing missing indexes or inefficient query plans that don't show up on small dev datasets. 
Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE on the production query (not dev) to see the actual execution plan — look for full table scans or index misses. Check if production statistics are 
stale (ANALYZE not run recently), causing the query planner to choose a bad plan. Check for lock contention in production from concurrent writes that don't exist in dev. 
Check hardware/resource differences (disk I/O, CPU, memory allocated to the DB) between environments. Check if production has different indexes than dev (schema drift).
Also verify if production is under concurrent load competing for I/O/connections at query time, unlike an idle dev DB.

7. A production issue happens only under heavy traffic and cannot be reproduced locally. What is your debugging approach?
Since it's not reproducible locally, rely on production observability: analyze APM traces and metrics (latency, error rate, resource usage) captured during the incident 
window to spot patterns — timeouts, GC pauses, connection pool exhaustion. Use distributed tracing with trace IDs to inspect actual failing requests from that time. 
Review logs (structured, with trace IDs) for errors/warnings correlated with the traffic spike. Check for race conditions or concurrency bugs that only manifest 
with many simultaneous threads (deadlocks, shared mutable state issues) — these are the classic causes of "works with 1 user, fails with 10,000." Attempt to reproduce with 
load testing (JMeter/Gatling) simulating similar concurrency in a staging environment. If still elusive, add targeted logging/feature flags to capture more detail on the 
next occurrence without a full redeploy.

Fault Tolerance & Resilience Q&A
8. When would you use a Circuit Breaker, Retry, Timeout, and Bulkhead pattern?
Timeout: Always use as the first line of defense — set on every network call (HTTP, DB) so a thread never waits indefinitely for a slow/unresponsive dependency.
Retry: Use for transient, short-lived failures (network blip, momentary unavailability) where the same request is likely to succeed on a second attempt. Pair with 
exponential backoff + jitter, and only retry idempotent operations to avoid duplicate side effects.
Circuit Breaker: Use when a dependency is repeatedly failing or consistently slow — it stops sending requests after a failure threshold, giving the downstream service
time to recover, and periodically tests recovery (half-open state) instead of hammering it with retries.
Bulkhead: Use to isolate resources (thread pools/connection pools) per dependency, so a slow/failing downstream service can't exhaust all threads and take down calls to
unrelated, healthy services.
Typically all four are combined: timeout bounds each call, retry handles blips, circuit breaker handles sustained failures, and bulkhead contains the blast radius — 
together forming a full resilience strategy.

9. Redis is down. Should your application also go down? How would you design the fallback?
No — the application should degrade gracefully, not go down entirely, unless Redis is absolutely critical to core functionality (e.g., session store with no alternative).
Design approach:
Circuit breaker around Redis calls: detect failures quickly and stop hammering a down Redis instance.
Fallback to source of truth: if Redis is used as a cache, fall back to querying the database directly (with a short timeout) instead of failing the request — accept higher 
latency temporarily rather than an outage.
Fallback to default/stale data: for non-critical reads, serve last-known-good or default values instead of failing.
Local in-memory cache (Caffeine) as a secondary cache layer to reduce DB load during Redis downtime.
Async/non-blocking calls: don't let Redis unavailability block the main thread — timeout fast and move on.
High availability: use Redis Sentinel or Redis Cluster to minimize the chance of full downtime in the first place.

Database & Data Consistency Q&A
10. Your database connection pool is exhausted in production. What could be the possible reasons?

Slow/long-running queries holding connections longer than expected (missing indexes, large scans, lock waits).
Connection leaks — code not closing/releasing connections properly (missing try-with-resources, exceptions skipping close()).
Undersized pool for actual traffic/concurrency — pool size not scaled with instance count or load.
Long transactions — connections held open across multiple operations, business logic, or external calls instead of being scoped tightly.
Sudden traffic spike exceeding normal capacity, especially without rate limiting.
Downstream slowness — a slow external call inside a transaction holds the DB connection idle while waiting.
Deadlocks/lock contention — transactions blocked waiting on locks, occupying connections without progressing.
Connections not returned after failures — exceptions during query execution not properly releasing the connection back to the pool.

Fix: identify via HikariCP metrics/leak detection, optimize slow queries, tighten transaction scope, and right-size the pool based on load testing.

11. How would you handle duplicate payment requests caused by retries or multiple button clicks?
Use an idempotency key — the client generates a unique key (UUID) per logical payment attempt and sends it in the request header. The server checks if that key
has already been processed: if yes, it returns the original cached response instead of reprocessing the payment; if no, it processes the payment and stores the 
result against that key. Enforce this with a unique DB constraint on the idempotency key so concurrent duplicate requests can't both succeed — the second insert 
fails and the response is fetched from the first. On the frontend, also disable the submit button after the first click as a UX-level safeguard (not a substitute 
for backend idempotency). Set an expiry on stored keys (e.g., 24 hours) to bound storage growth while covering realistic retry windows.

12. Where would you store an idempotency key, and how would you handle concurrent requests with the same key?
Storage: Store idempotency keys in a fast, shared store like Redis (with TTL for auto-expiry) for quick lookups, or in a database table with a unique constraint on 
the key column if you need strong durability/consistency (common for payments). Redis is preferred for speed; DB is preferred when the key's outcome must survive Redis failures.
Handling concurrent requests with the same key:
Use SETNX in Redis (set-if-not-exists) as an atomic lock — the first request acquires it and proceeds; concurrent duplicates see the key already exists and either wait (poll) or 
immediately return a "processing" status.
In DB, the unique constraint ensures only one insert succeeds; the second gets a constraint violation, catches it, and fetches/returns the first request's stored result.
Once processing completes, store the response against the key so subsequent duplicate calls (even after completion) return the same cached result instead of reprocessing.

13. Your service receives the same request twice because of a network retry. How would you guarantee consistent results?
This is solved by making the operation idempotent, so processing it twice produces the same end state as processing it once:

Idempotency key: Client sends a unique request ID; server checks if it's already been processed and returns the stored result for duplicates instead of re-executing business logic.
Unique constraints: At the DB level, use unique keys (e.g., on order_id + request_id) so a duplicate insert fails safely and can be handled gracefully rather than creating a 
second record.
Conditional updates: Use "update where status = X" patterns so a second identical request finds the state already changed and becomes a safe no-op.
Natural idempotency: Design operations to be idempotent by nature where possible — e.g., "set balance to $100" instead of "add $10," so repeating it doesn't compound the effect.
Combined, these ensure that regardless of how many times a request is retried over the network, the final system state remains correct and consistent.

Caching Q&A
14. How would you solve the cache invalidation problem when multiple service instances are running?
The core issue: if each instance uses a local in-memory cache (Caffeine/Guava), invalidating on one instance doesn't clear it on the others, causing stale data across the fleet.
Solutions:
Centralized cache (Redis): Use a shared distributed cache instead of local caches — all instances read/write the same cache, so a single invalidation (delete/update) is instantly 
visible to everyone. This is the most common and simplest fix.
Cache invalidation via Pub/Sub: If local caches are still needed for speed, use Redis Pub/Sub or Kafka to broadcast an "invalidate key X" event to all instances when data changes, 
so each instance clears its local entry.
TTL-based expiry: Set a reasonable time-to-live on cache entries as a safety net, ensuring stale data self-corrects within a bounded window even if an explicit invalidation is missed.
Write-through/Cache-aside pattern: Update the cache (or invalidate it) in the same transaction/step as the DB write, ensuring consistency at the point of change rather than relying 
on background sync.
Versioned cache keys: Include a version/timestamp in the cache key (e.g., user:123:v5); on update, bump the version so old cached entries are naturally bypassed without explicit deletion.
In practice, a shared Redis cache combined with a short TTL covers most cases simply and reliably; Pub/Sub invalidation is added only when local caching is required for extreme 
low-latency needs.

15. How would you process a 5GB CSV file without causing an Out Of Memory Error?
The core principle: never load the entire file into memory at once — stream and process it incrementally so memory usage stays constant (O(1)) regardless of file size, instead of growing 
with the file (O(n)).
1. Stream the file, don't load it fully
Use a buffered reader (BufferedReader in Java, or similar streaming APIs) to read one line at a time, process it, and discard it — rather than loading all rows into a List or 
in-memory collection.
2. Use streaming CSV parsers
Use libraries like Apache Commons CSV or OpenCSV in streaming mode, which parse records one at a time instead of loading the whole file into memory (avoid loading it fully into 
a DataFrame for the same reason).
3. Process in batches
Process records in small batches (e.g., 1,000 rows at a time) and write/commit each batch incrementally to the database, instead of accumulating everything before writing.
4. Use a framework built for this
For large-scale or repeatable jobs, use Spring Batch — it natively handles:
Chunk-oriented processing (ItemReader → ItemProcessor → ItemWriter)
Checkpointing (resume from where it failed)
Restart on failure without reprocessing everything
5. Parallelize if needed
Split the file into smaller parts and process them concurrently across multiple threads or worker instances to speed up processing.
6. Tune the JVM (secondary measure)
Increasing JVM heap size can help as a buffer, but it's not the fix — streaming is what actually solves the OOM problem, not more memory.

