16. When would you choose synchronous communication over asynchronous messaging between microservices?
Choose synchronous (REST/gRPC) when the caller needs an immediate response to proceed — e.g., validating a user during login, checking inventory availability before confirming an order,
or any real-time query where the result directly drives the next UI/logic step. It's simpler to implement, debug, and trace, and suits low-latency, request-response interactions between 
tightly coupled internal services.
Choose asynchronous (Kafka/RabbitMQ) when operations can be decoupled and processed independently — e.g., sending notifications, updating analytics, or triggering downstream
workflows — improving resilience, scalability, and fault tolerance since services aren't blocked waiting on each other.

17. What happens if a Kafka consumer processes a message successfully but crashes before committing the offset?
Since the offset wasn't committed, Kafka still considers that message unacknowledged. When the consumer restarts (or a new consumer in the group takes over the partition), it resumes 
from the last committed offset, causing the already-processed message to be redelivered and reprocessed — this is Kafka's at-least-once delivery guarantee in action. This is why 
consumers must be designed to be idempotent: reprocessing the same message shouldn't cause incorrect side effects (e.g., duplicate charges). Using manual offset commits after successful 
processing (not before) minimizes this window, but can't eliminate it entirely — idempotency at the application level is the real safeguard.
18. How would you prevent the same Kafka event from being processed twice?

Idempotent consumers: Design processing logic so reprocessing has no additional effect — e.g., use upserts instead of inserts, or check a unique event ID before applying changes.
Deduplication store: Track processed message IDs (Kafka message key or a custom unique ID) in Redis/DB; before processing, check if the ID was already handled, and skip if so.
Database unique constraints: Enforce uniqueness on the event ID at the DB level so duplicate inserts fail safely and can be caught/ignored.
Transactional outbox + exactly-once semantics: Use Kafka's idempotent producer and transactional API (enable.idempotence=true, read-process-write transactions) to achieve effectively 
exactly-once processing within Kafka itself.
Combining idempotent design with a deduplication check is the most reliable, practical approach in production.

