Kubernetes & Containerization Q&A
19. Your Kubernetes pod keeps restarting, but application logs show no clear error. How would you debug it?
Run kubectl describe pod <pod-name> to check events — look for OOMKilled, CrashLoopBackOff, or Liveness probe failed reasons, which often explain restarts that don't appear in app logs. 
Check kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o yaml for exit codes (137 = OOM killed, 143 = SIGTERM). Check resource limits — if memory limits are too low, Kubernetes kills the container before 
the app logs an error. Review liveness/readiness probe configuration — a misconfigured probe (too aggressive timeout) can cause healthy pods to be killed. Check previous container 
logs with kubectl logs <pod> --previous. Also check node-level issues (disk pressure, node resource exhaustion) via kubectl describe node.

20. How would you deploy a new version of a Spring Boot service without downtime?
Use a rolling update (Kubernetes default Deployment strategy) — new pods with the updated version are gradually started while old pods are terminated, ensuring some instances are always
available to serve traffic. Configure readiness probes so Kubernetes only routes traffic to new pods once they're fully initialized (Spring context loaded, DB connections ready). 
Set maxUnavailable and maxSurge appropriately to control rollout pace. For safer releases, use blue-green deployment (switch traffic entirely once the new version is verified) or 
canary deployment (route a small % of traffic to the new version first, monitor, then gradually increase). Combine with backward-compatible DB migrations so old and new versions can 
coexist briefly during rollout.

21. What happens to in-flight requests during pod termination, and how would you handle graceful shutdown?
When Kubernetes terminates a pod, it sends a SIGTERM signal and starts a grace period (default 30s) before force-killing with SIGKILL. Without proper handling, in-flight requests can 
be abruptly cut off, causing errors for users.
Graceful shutdown approach:
Enable Spring Boot's graceful shutdown: server.shutdown=graceful with spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase=30s — this lets the app stop accepting new requests but finish processing 
existing ones.
Add a preStop hook in the pod spec to delay SIGTERM slightly, allowing the load balancer/service to deregister the pod first (avoiding new traffic being routed to a terminating pod).
Ensure the app closes DB connections, flushes logs, and completes in-progress transactions before fully exiting, all within the grace period.

22. How would you handle distributed transactions across multiple microservices?
Since each microservice owns its own database, a single ACID transaction can't span all of them. The standard solution is the Saga pattern — breaking the transaction into a sequence of 
local transactions, each committed independently and coordinated via events (choreography) or a central coordinator (orchestration). If a step fails, compensating transactions undo the 
preceding steps to restore consistency. Supplement this with the Outbox pattern (atomically persisting DB changes and events together) to avoid lost events, and idempotent consumers to 
safely handle retries/duplicates. Two-Phase Commit (2PC) is generally avoided in microservices due to its blocking nature and poor scalability.

23. When would you use the Saga pattern, and how would you handle compensation failures?
Use the Saga pattern whenever a business transaction spans multiple microservices with separate databases and needs eventual consistency without tightly coupling services — e.g., 
order → payment → inventory → shipping flows.
Handling compensation failures (when the rollback itself fails):

Retry with backoff: Compensating actions should be retried automatically, since failures are often transient.
Idempotent compensations: Design them so repeated execution is safe.
Dead Letter Queue: If compensation keeps failing, route it to a DLQ for manual intervention rather than leaving the system silently inconsistent.
Alerting: Notify ops immediately so a human can resolve the stuck state.
Persistent saga state: Track each step's status (via an orchestrator/state machine) so failed compensations can be resumed reliably after a restart, rather than lost.
